<h1>Jeanty & Washington: The Raiders' Two <em>Different</em> Athletes</h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📖 7 min read</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">⚡ Sprint Speed</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏈 Backfield Breakdown</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Raiders head coach Klint Kubiak is building the same two-back system in Las Vegas that helped Seattle win it all last year — this time around Ashton Jeanty and rookie Mike Washington Jr. One of them has an elite, officially tested combine profile. The other has never run a 40-yard dash in his life. Here's how the two backs actually compare.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">
<div class="callout" style="background:var(--surface);border-color:var(--accent);">
<div class="callout-icon">⚡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Mike Washington Jr. ran a <strong>4.33-second 40</strong> at the 2026 combine, a 99th-percentile weight-adjusted mark for running backs. Ashton Jeanty has <strong>never run an official 40</strong> — he skipped both the 2025 combine and his Boise State pro day. With Jeanty dealing with an ankle injury, Washington ran with the first team in the preseason finale and gained 49 yards on 8 carries (6.1 YPC).</div>
</div>
<h2>Why the Raiders Are Building a Two-Back System</h2>
<p>Ashton Jeanty's rookie season was a workload story more than a talent question. Raiders running backs carried the ball 307 times in 2025, and Jeanty accounted for 266 of them — <strong>86.6%</strong> of the team's total rushing attempts. He played 770 of the Raiders' 994 offensive snaps, a 77.5% snap share that buried him in touches with almost no relief.</p>
<p>New head coach Klint Kubiak ran a similar two-back rotation last year in Seattle, splitting carries between Kenneth Walker III (221 carries, 1,027 yards, 4.6 YPC) and Zach Charbonnet (184 carries, 730 yards, 12 TDs) en route to a Super Bowl. He's bringing that structure to Las Vegas, pairing Jeanty with fourth-round rookie Mike Washington Jr. out of Arkansas. "I think that they're both going to prepare to play a lot of football," Kubiak said. "We've got two guys that we're really excited about."</p>
<h2>The Combine Numbers: A Study in Contrasts</h2>
<p>This isn't a clean athletic comparison, because one of these backs simply doesn't have an official testing number. Here's what exists on paper for each:</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Ashton Jeanty</th>
<th>Mike Washington Jr.</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>School</td><td>Boise State</td><td>Arkansas</td></tr>
<tr><td>Draft Status</td><td>No. 6 overall, 2025</td><td>4th round, 2026</td></tr>
<tr><td>Combine Height/Weight</td><td>5'8", 211 lbs</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr class="elite"><td>40-Yard Dash</td><td>Did not test</td><td>4.33s (99th %ile weight-adj.)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vertical / Broad Jump</td><td>Did not test</td><td>Not publicly tested</td></tr>
<tr><td>College Rushing Style</td><td>193 gap / 181 zone runs</td><td>90 gap / 72 zone runs</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Jeanty skipped every workout drill at the 2025 combine and then declined the 40, vertical, and bench press at his own Boise State pro day, betting that game tape alone would carry his stock. It worked — he still went No. 6 overall on the strength of a junior season with 2,601 rushing yards and 29 touchdowns, the second-highest single-season rushing total in FBS history. He simply has no official testing number to compare against Washington's blazing 4.33.</p>
<p>Washington, by contrast, ran the ninth-fastest 40 time of any player at the 2026 combine — faster than most wide receivers and defensive backs, let alone running backs. See how that stacks up against every position group in our <a href="/fastest-nfl-rookies-2026-training-camp">full breakdown of the fastest 2026 rookies</a>.</p>
