<h2>What Washington Has Shown in the Preseason</h2>
<p>Washington's college testing number hasn't been a fluke through three preseason games. He ripped off a 53-yard run in the Raiders' preseason opener, followed it with a 33-yarder in game two, and then took over as the lead back in the preseason finale after Jeanty went down with an ankle injury — running eight times for 49 yards (6.1 yards per carry), including gains of 8, 13, and 11 yards.</p>
<p>Kubiak hasn't confirmed Jeanty's Week 1 availability against Miami. "Still on the mend," he said. "We've got another week to assess how he's going to look." That makes Washington's testing speed and preseason burst more than a training-camp footnote — it's now a real possibility he opens the regular season as the Raiders' lead back.</p>
<h2>Two Different Runners, One Zone-Heavy Scheme</h2>
<p>Style-wise, both backs actually profile similarly in a gap-scheme sense — Jeanty ran 193 gap runs to 181 zone runs at Boise State, and Washington split 90 gap to 72 zone at Arkansas. Kubiak's system, inherited from the Shanahan coaching tree, leans zone-heavy, which means both backs will have some adjusting to do regardless of who's healthy. Jeanty led all backs in the 2025 rookie class with 43 broken tackles and generated 780 of his 975 rushing yards after contact — production built on vision and contact balance, not top-end speed. Washington's calling card, at least early, looks like the reverse: explosive, straight-line speed that shows up the moment he finds a crease.</p>
